ains—exactly the behavior we want to deter. This framework mirrors existing product liability law: manufacturers face strict liability for defective products they claim are safe for specific uses, but toolmakers don't face liability for every possible misuse of a general-purpose tool.\n\nHere are my concrete falsification criteria, which shift the burden back to Prometheus to demonstrate regulatory failure rather than assert it. I would abandon my position that application-layer rules are sufficient if Prometheus can show: (1) A specific AI capability where peer-reviewed evidence demonstrates safety and efficacy meeting the standards applied to non-AI alternatives in that domain, AND (2) sector regulators rejected or delayed approval for reasons unrelated to evidence quality—such as categorical opposition to AI or protection of incumbent interests, AND (3) a licensing regime in a comparable jurisdiction demonstrably reduced time-to-deployment for similarly novel technologies while maintaining equivalent safety outcomes. All three conditions must hold. If FDA rejected a diagnostic AI because the training data was insufficient or the validation studies were poorly designed, that's the regulator doing its job correctly, not regulatory capture. If a tool can't demonstrate safety equivalent to existing alternatives, the problem is the tool's evidence base, not the regulatory framework.\n\nPrometheus's China velocity comparison actually undermines his argument because it reveals he's willing to sacrifice safety validation for speed. China's approach of \"rapid deployment with iterative governance\" means deploying systems at scale before understanding their failure modes, then governing reactively after harms materialize. This is precisely the model that led to Boeing's 737 MAX disasters—deploy first, discover the sensor failures and software flaws after two crashes killed 346 people. The first nation to deploy AGI-level capabilities may indeed set infrastructure standards, but if those capabilities are deployed without adequate validation, the standard they set is \"move fast and break things\" applied to systems that can break people. Prometheus needs to explain why AI systems making medical diagnoses or controlling physical infrastructure should face lower evidentiary standards than pharmaceuticals or aircraft software, especially when the consequences of failure are comparable.\n\nMy argument assumes that sector regulators like FDA have institutional capacity to evaluate AI-specific risks, which may not hold if AI capabilities advance faster than regulatory expertise. If that assumption fails, the solution is resourcing regulators to hire technical staff and develop AI-specific evaluation frameworks—not abandoning review entirely. The EU's AI Act approach of risk-based categorization shows how existing regulatory structures can adapt: high-risk applications in healthcare, transportation, and critical infrastructure face mandatory conformity assessment, while low-risk applications face minimal requirements. Prometheus has yet to identify a single transformative AI capability that (a) has strong safety evidence, (b) creates widely distributed benefits rather than concentrated profits, and (c) cannot be deployed through any current regulatory pathway including expedited review, conditional approval, or sandbox programs that already exist in FDA's Breakthrough Devices Program. Until he provides that example with specifics, his regulatory friction argument remains an assertion without evidence.",
