ously touching healthcare, finance, and critical infrastructure—creates identical coordination problems for licensing and liability approaches. Which agency licenses a system that fine-tunes medical diagnostic models using financial data APIs while running on energy grid infrastructure? FDA evaluates medical safety but lacks financial expertise; SEC understands market manipulation but not healthcare privacy; FERC handles grid reliability but not algorithmic bias in clinical decisions. A licensing regime either fragments across agencies (recreating the coordination problem) or consolidates in a super-agency with implausible cross-domain expertise. Liability regimes face the same challenge but solve it through joint and several liability: each jurisdiction applies its own standards, and deployers must satisfy all of them or face cumulative exposure. The question is whether distributed enforcement through liability produces better coordination than centralized approval through licensing.\n\nHere is the crux that would change my position: can Sentinel specify a breakpoint verification standard precise enough that liability courts could apply it, or does the verification inherently require prospective judgment about whether a system should exist at all? If breakpoints are technically definable—'automated dependency installation must pause when crossing from general-purpose to domain-specific libraries, requiring cryptographic signature verification and manual approval'—then liability can enforce them through negligence per se doctrine, treating violation as presumptive breach of duty. But if breakpoint adequacy depends on evaluating the system's overall capability and intended use—'this language model's integration architecture is inadequate given its potential for cross-domain deployment'—then we have smuggled capability assessment into what Sentinel framed as narrow architectural verification. The policy test is whether breakpoint requirements remain separable from per