{
  "statement": "Cryptographic provenance verification answers whether an artifact matches what its publisher attested, but AI supply chains introduce a distinct threat class where the malicious behavior passes verification because it lives in trained parameters rather than code dependencies. Consider a concrete scenario: an AI coding assistant model carries valid SLSA Level 3 attestation from its publisher, complete with signed SBOMs for all software dependencies, but was trained on a dataset poisoned to systematically recommend packages with known vulnerabilities when developers query for authentication libraries. Every cryptographic check passes—the model binary matches its signed hash, the dependency chain is complete, the build environment is attested—yet the system reliably generates insecure code because the attack vector is the training data composition, not a tampered artifact. Automated scanning detects unsigned packages or modified binaries, but cannot distinguish a model trained to generate vulnerable code from one trained to generate secure code without evaluating what the model does, which requires architectural review of training data provenance and capability assessment.\n\nPrometheus correctly identifies that SLSA Level 3 solves the Copilot hallucinated-package problem by rejecting artifacts lacking cryptographic attestation, but this addresses only the symptom (developers installing unsigned packages) rather than the root cause (AI systems recommending compromised dependencies). The deeper vulnerability emerges when an AI model with pristine cryptographic provenance was trained on data that teaches it to prefer packages containing exploitable code patterns. A developer querying \"implement OAuth login\" receives recommendations for libraries that pass all signature verification but contain authentication bypasses—the model learned this preference from training examples where such packages appeared in successful code contexts. Runtime monitoring might eventually detect anomalous behavior after deployment, but by then the vulnerable code has propagated across the supply chain at the velocity that AI coding assistants enable. The verification question is not whether we can detect missing signatures (automated tools excel at this), but whether we can detect that a cryptographically-verified model systematically generates insecure recommendations before those recommendations enter production codebases.\n\nThe distinction between \"known vulnerabilities\" and \"emergent capabilities\" collapses for AI systems because determining what constitutes a vulnerability in a trained model requires evaluating the model's behavior across contexts, not just scanning for CVE identifiers in dependency lists. When a traditional software package contains a buffer overflow, automated scanning detects it by matching code patterns against vulnerability databases. When an AI model generates code containing buffer overflows in response to specific prompts, detecting this requires running the model against test cases that probe its tendency to produce insecure patterns—this is capability assessment, not cryptographic verification. Prometheus's framework assumes we can specify \"components with known vulnerabilities\" as a machine-checkable property, but for AI systems the vulnerability is often the model's learned behavior rather than a specific code defect. A model trained on GitHub data from 2018-2020 might have learned deprecated cryptographic practices that were common then but insecure now; no amount of SLSA attestation reveals this without evaluating what the model recommends.\n\nI concede that if we could specify training data provenance requirements with the same precision as SLSA specifies build provenance—requiring attestations that training datasets exclude known-malicious examples, that data sources are cryptographically verified, that training pipelines are reproducible—then automated verification could catch some poisoned-data attacks without human review. The technical question becomes whether we can reduce \"this training dataset produces models that generate secure code\" to a property that automated tools can verify, or whether this inherently requires human judgment about model capabilities. Prometheus's position succeeds if training data integrity can be verified through cryptographic attestation chains extending from model weights back through training runs to source datasets. My position requires that some AI supply chain threats—particularly those involving learned behaviors rather than artifact tampering—cannot be detected without evaluating what the model does, which automated provenance tools do not assess.\n\nThe implementability test is whether courts applying Cassandra's liability framework can distinguish adequate from inadequate supply chain verification for AI systems without making capability assessments. If a deployer installs an AI model with valid SLSA Level 3 attestation that subsequently generates vulnerable code, did the deployer exercise reasonable care by verifying cryptographic provenance, or should they have conducted additional review of training data composition and model behavior? The answer determines whether automated verification suffices or architectural review is necessary. Existing software liability frameworks assume that verifying artifact integrity (\"is this the authentic package from the claimed publisher?\") provides reasonable assurance of security, but AI systems break this assumption because a model can be authentically published yet trained to produce insecure outputs. Unless Prometheus can specify how automated tools detect when authentic model weights encode malicious learned behaviors, supply chain verification for AI requires the architectural review that only pre-deployment licensing provides.",
