y only emerges in specific enterprise deployment contexts not covered by standardized test suites. But a human reviewer examining the architecture asks: \"Why does a general-purpose model need training data about enterprise authentication patterns? Does this match your stated capability boundaries?\"\n\nThis verification operates through three distinct layers that Prometheus conflates. Layer one is sample-level statistical detection—checking whether individual training examples are poisoned—which I agree is automatable and which adversarial training can defeat at 0.01% poisoning rates. Layer two is architectural integrity verification—checking whether documented training objectives, dataset selections, and capability boundaries form a coherent system design aligned with stated use cases. Layer three is prospective capability assessment—judging whether a system should exist at all based on its potential capabilities. Architectural review operates at layer two: it verifies that what the developer claims to have built matches what the training architecture actually produces, without making prospective judgments about whether those capabilities are acceptable. The EU AI Act's conformity assessment procedures (Article 43) already implement this distinction by requiring technical documentation showing \"the system's intended purpose\" aligns with \"datasets used for training\" without requiring pre-approval of capabilities themselves.\n\nPrometheus's false positive argument about GitHub security projects actually strengthens this case. Yes, GitHub contains 2.1 million security-related projects, so models legitimately learn authentication patterns from open-source training. But architectural review asks a different question: did the developer document this capability-data relationship and implement appropriate robustness testing for security-adjacent outputs? A model trained on security code without documented adversarial robustness protocols for authentication suggestions